.TH SDL_CreateThread 3 "SDL 3.5.0" "Simple Directmedia Layer" "SDL3 FUNCTIONS"
.SH NAME
SDL_CreateThread \- Create a new thread with a default stack size.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <SDL3/SDL_thread.h>
.PP
.BI "SDL_Thread * SDL_CreateThread(SDL_ThreadFunction fn, const char *name, void *data);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
This is a convenience function, equivalent to calling
.BR SDL_CreateThreadWithProperties
with the following properties set:
.PP

\(bu
.BR SDL_PROP_THREAD_CREATE_ENTRY_FUNCTION_POINTER :
.BR fn
.PP
\(bu
.BR SDL_PROP_THREAD_CREATE_NAME_STRING :
.BR name
.PP
\(bu
.BR SDL_PROP_THREAD_CREATE_USERDATA_POINTER :
.BR data
.PP
Note that this \(dqfunction\(dq is actually a macro that calls an internal function with two extra parameters not listed here; they are hidden through preprocessor macros and are needed to support various C runtimes at the point of the function call\[char46] Language bindings that aren\(aqt using the C headers will need to deal with this\[char46]
.PP
Usually, apps should just call this function the same way on every platform and let the macros hide the details\[char46]

.SH FUNCTION PARAMETERS
.TP
.I fn
the
.BR SDL_ThreadFunction
function to call in the new thread\[char46]
.TP
.I name
the name of the thread\[char46]
.TP
.I data
a pointer that is passed to
.BR fn \[char46]

.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns an opaque pointer to the new thread object on success, NULL if the new thread could not be created; call
.BR SDL_GetError ()
for more information\[char46]

.SH THREAD SAFETY
It is safe to call this function from any thread\[char46]

.SH AVAILABILITY
This function is available since SDL 3\[char46]2\[char46]0\[char46]

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR SDL_CreateThreadWithProperties (3),
.BR SDL_WaitThread (3)
